## What happened

In .NET 11, **DSA (Digital Signature Algorithm) was removed from macOS**. Calling `DSA.Create()` now throws at runtime on macOS. Windows and Linux are unaffected.

## How to fix

Since you're only **verifying** legacy signatures (not generating new ones), you have two options:

### Option 1: Use ECDSA/RSA for new work, gate DSA to non-macOS

If you must still verify existing DSA signatures on macOS, this isn't possible with the built-in APIs anymore. You'd need a third-party library (e.g., BouncyCastle).

### Option 2: Replace DSA verification entirely

If you can re-sign or migrate away from DSA signatures:

```csharp
// Before (.NET 10) — breaks on macOS in .NET 11
using var dsa = DSA.Create();
dsa.ImportParameters(legacyPublicKey);
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);

// After — use ECDsa (requires new signatures)
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
ecdsa.ImportParameters(newPublicKey);
bool valid = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

### Option 3: Platform guard (if macOS verification isn't required)

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // verify legacy DSA signature
}
```

**Recommended path:** If you need cross-platform DSA verification, use **BouncyCastle** (`Portable.BouncyCastle` NuGet package) which provides a managed DSA implementation independent of the OS. Otherwise, migrate signatures to ECDSA or RSA.
